# Progress ring

A progress ring (or circular progress bar) provides visual feedback on the status of a task. Unlike a linear progress bar, its circular shape is ideal for dashboards, card components, or anywhere space is constrained.

This guide implements a progress ring by:

- Using the native `<progress>` element as the semantic foundation to ensure the component is accessible to screen readers and keyboard users out-of-the-box.
- Styling the component with `conic-gradient()` and `mask-image`, allowing for a fully responsive and themeable ring without the complexity of SVG path manipulation.
- Leveraging CSS Custom Properties and `@property` to enable smooth, GPU-accelerated transitions of the progress fill.

This approach is preferred over SVG-only solutions because it uses the semantic `<progress>` element rather than ARIA, and more easily integrates with existing layout, design systems and typography.

See the `spinner` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "spinner"`) for handling indeterminate loading states.

## Implementation

### 1. Markup

Use a wrapper to hold both the visual ring and the optional center content. The `<progress>` element remains the semantic source of truth. Use a utility class to visually hide the native progress bar while keeping it accessible.

```html
<div class="progress-ring-wrapper">
  <progress value="75" max="100" aria-label="Task progress" class="progress-ring"></progress>
  <!-- Optional: Content to display in the center. Hidden from assistive
       technologies because it repeats the value of the <progress> element. -->
  <div class="progress-ring-content" aria-hidden="true">
    75%
  </div>
</div>
```

Hide the center content from assistive technologies with `aria-hidden="true"`. The `<progress>` element already exposes its value, so screen readers would otherwise announce it twice.

If the center content shows the value in another form, such as "3 of 5 files" or a percentage, set the same text in `aria-valuetext` on the `<progress>` element whenever you update it, otherwise screen readers only get the number.

### 2. Styles

#### Hiding Native UI
To style the `<progress>` element as a progress ring, first hide the default browser styling for progress bars.

```css
/* Hide native bars */
progress.progress-ring::-webkit-progress-bar {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.progress-ring::-webkit-progress-value {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.progress-ring::-moz-progress-bar {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.progress-ring::slider-fill {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
```

#### Container and Ring

The wrapper provides the positioning context. The `<progress>` element handles the visual gradient. Forced colors mode removes background gradients, so in `@media (forced-colors: active)`, opt the ring out with `forced-color-adjust: none` and draw it with system colors, or the ring disappears.

```css
.progress-ring-wrapper {
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
}

progress.progress-ring {
  --size: 150px;
  --thickness: 16px;
  --track-color: #f1f5f9;
  --fill-color: #3b82f6;
  --value: attr(value type(<number>));
  width: var(--size);
  height: var(--size);

  transition: --value 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
  border-radius: 50%;

  background: conic-gradient(
    var(--fill-color) calc(var(--value) * 1%),
    var(--track-color) 0
  );

  /* MANDATORY: Clip the background to the border-area. */
  background-clip: border-area;
  border: var(--thickness) solid transparent;
  background-origin: border-box;

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    /* Keeps the gradient, which forced colors mode removes, and draws it in system colors */
    forced-color-adjust: none;
    --fill-color: CanvasText;
    --track-color: Canvas;
  }
}

.progress-ring-content {
  /* Positioned in the center of the wrapper */
  position: absolute;
}
```

You can also use a `radial-gradient` to make rounded end caps.

#### Enable smooth transitions with `@property`

To animate the progress ring smoothly when the value changes, register `--value` as a numeric custom property. 

Users with motion sensitivities may find the transition between values disorienting. Respect the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query by having a 0 second (immediate) duration by default, and setting a longer time for users with no preference.


```css
@property --value {
  syntax: '<number>';
  inherits: true;
  initial-value: 0;
}

progress.progress-ring {
  transition: --value 0s ease-in-out;
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    transition-duration: 0.4s;
  }
}

```

### 3. Progress Updates

Update the `value` attribute on the `<progress>` element and the text content of `.progress-ring-content` (if displaying a percentage) whenever the value changes.

### 4. Optional Success State

You can use the CSS attribute selector to automatically update the ring's appearance (e.g., changing the color to green) when the task reaches completion.

```css
/* Change the fill color to green when the progress reaches 100% */
progress.progress-ring[value="100"] {
  --fill-color: #10b981;
}
```

## Fallback strategies

Do not add a fallback value inside the `<progress>` element. It is not used by assistive technology and ignored by all modern browsers.

#### Animation fallback

Baseline status for Registered custom properties: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2024-07-09.
Supported by: Chrome 85 (Aug 2020), Edge 85 (Aug 2020), Firefox 128 (Jul 2024), and Safari 16.4 (Mar 2023).

If `@property` is not supported, the ring will jump to the new value instantly instead of transitioning smoothly. In most cases this is fine, and does not break any functionality.

If the transition is absolutely necessary, you can check for `@property` support and use a `requestAnimationFrame()` loop to interpolate `--value` in older browsers.

Browser support for background-clip: border-area: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 150 (Jun 2026), Edge 150 (Jul 2026), and Safari 18.2 (Dec 2024).
Unsupported in: Firefox.

For browsers that don't yet support `background-clip: border-area`, fall back to a `mask-image` to hollow out the center of the `<progress>` element.

```css
@supports not (background-clip: border-area) {
  mask-image: radial-gradient(
    transparent calc(50% - var(--thickness)),
    black calc(50% - var(--thickness) + 0.5px)
  );
  border: 0;
}
```

Browser support for attr(): Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 133 (Feb 2025), Edge 133 (Feb 2025), and Firefox 155 (Sep 2026).
Unsupported in: Safari.

For browsers that don't support the `attr()` CSS function for any property, use a `MutationObserver` to automatically sync the `value` attribute to the `--value` custom property.

```js
if (!CSS.supports("width: attr(value type(<number>))")) {
  const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {
    progress.style.setProperty("--value", progress.getAttribute("value"));
  });
  observer.observe(progress, {
    attributes: true,
    attributeFilter: ["value"],
  });
}
```

# Build a Styled Progress Bar

The `<progress>` element is the semantic way to represent the completion progress of a task, like downloading or uploading information or completing part of a multi-step process. Using the native element helps with accessibility, as screen readers have an implicit `role="progressbar"` and can announce values.

This guide implements a fully styled horizontal progress bar with style customizations.

## Progress bar or progress ring?

- If space is constrained or a square aspect ratio is preferable, consider using a circular progress bar (progress ring): `progress-ring` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "progress-ring"`)
- Prefer spinners for most indeterminate progress scenarios (`spinner` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "spinner"`)). Use indeterminate progress bars when the indeterminate state is temporary and will soon be replaced by a determinate progress bar (e.g. waiting for metadata around an upload before a progress % can be shown)

## Markup

1.  **Use a native `<progress>` element**: It provides built-in accessibility and platform-consistent behavior.
2. Use `max` if a scale different from the default 0-1 scale is needed.
3. For a specific progress value, set a `value` attribute. Omit `value` for an indeterminate state (e.g. before more concrete feedback starts).
4. Use `<label for="...">`, `aria-labelledby`, or `aria-label` to associate the progress element with a label.

## What type of customization is needed?

For limited styling, these properties are sufficient:
- `accent-color` to change the color of the progress bar
- `width` / `inline-size` to change the width of the progress bar
- `height`/`block-size` applies inconsistently — test first
- `writing-mode: vertical-lr` for vertical bars.
- Effects that apply after painting: `opacity`, `filter`, `clip-path`, masks, transforms, `box-shadow`, etc.

If limited styling suffices, skip the next section.

WARNING: Certain properties (e.g. `border-radius`, `background`, `border`) will silently disable the default progress bar styling. If you need styling beyond the properties above, you must implement fully custom styling as described in the next section. Do NOT apply more extensive styling "just in case it works".

## Fully custom styling

### 1. Basic setup

- MANDATORY: Use `appearance: none` to enable custom styling.
- Reset UA borders with `border: 0 solid`
- To clip the active bar to the element’s border-radius, use `overflow: clip` OR apply the same `border-radius` to the bar. Do NOT use `border-radius: inherit` on the bar, which depends on the specific structure of elements in the shadow DOM.

```css
progress {
  /* Reset default browser styles */
  appearance: none;
  border: 0 solid;
  /* Clip bar to element’s border-radius  */
  overflow: clip;
}
```

### 2. Styling

- Style the track on the `progress` element itself.
- Use the appropriate browser-targeted pseudo-element to target the progress bar itself: `::-webkit-progress-value` for WebKit/Blink and `::-moz-progress-bar` for Firefox.
- MANDATORY: Do NOT group `-webkit-` and `-moz-` pseudo-elements in the same rule. The rule will be deemed invalid and will be ignored. `:is()` and `:where()` do not help here, as they don't support pseudo-elements.
- Each browser-targeted pseudo-element should appear exactly once. State-specific customizations (such as the indeterminate segment size and animation) should be routed through custom properties set on the `progress` element itself.
- Optional: Transition `inline-size` on the fill so determinate value changes animate instead of jumping. Firefox sizes the fill in layout and ignores it, which is harmless.

```css
progress {
  --progress-bar-radius: 1e5px;
  border-radius: var(--progress-bar-radius);
  /* Apply color for custom progress track (background) */
  background: var(--progress-bar-track, var(--color-neutral-90));
  /* Subtle depth on the empty part of the track */
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.1);
}

/* Let <progress> styling show through */
progress::-webkit-progress-bar {
  background: transparent;
}

/* Webkit, Chrome */
progress::-webkit-progress-value {
  background: var(--progress-bar-fill, var(--accent-color));
  border-radius: var(--progress-bar-radius);
  transition: inline-size 0.3s;
}

/* Firefox */
progress::-moz-progress-bar {
  background: var(--progress-bar-fill, var(--accent-color));
  border-radius: var(--progress-bar-radius);
  transition: inline-size 0.3s;
}
```

### 3. Indeterminate state styling

**Add a custom animation for the indeterminate state:** Turn the fill pseudo-element into a short segment and slide it along the track.
- Style the fill itself; do NOT recreate it as a background on `progress`.
- The indeterminate fill exists only through its animation: the keyframes set its length with a real `inline-size` (this beats Chromium's UA inline `inline-size`) and moves it with `translate`. Do NOT use `scale` for the length; it distorts any pattern painted on the fill.
- `translate` percentages resolve against the fill's own width, so express positions in track units instead: make `progress` an inline-size container and use `cqi` in the keyframes. Include `margin-right: auto` in `from, to` so Chromium lays the fill out at the physical left in RTL too.
- Use transforms, not margins, to move the fill.
  - Transforms keep the animation in the compositor (unless `inline-size` is also being animated)
  - Margins break in RTL, for two separate reasons:
    - Firefox positions the indeterminate fill using only its physical left margin, so `margin-inline-start` has no effect in RTL and the fill never moves.
    - In Chromium and WebKit, which lay the fill out as a normal block, once a margin pushes the fill past the end of the track the margins add up to more than the track. Standard CSS resolves that by ignoring `margin-left` in RTL, so the segment jumps to the edge instead of sliding off it.
  - `translate` is applied after layout, so neither problem applies.
- To mirror the motion in RTL (as Chrome's native bar does), reverse the animation under `:dir(rtl)`:

```css
progress:dir(rtl) {
  --progress-bar-indeterminate-animation: progress-bar-indeterminate var(--progress-bar-indeterminate-animation-duration) infinite linear reverse;
}
```

- Define the public `--progress-bar-indeterminate-*` properties on `progress` (their name already scopes them to the state) so consumers can override them without knowing about `:indeterminate`.
- To vary the segment length (e.g. the leading edge races ahead and the trailing edge catches up), set `--progress-bar-indeterminate-animation` to two concurrent animations: one whose keyframes animate `translate` in `cqi` (position) and one whose keyframes animate `inline-size` (length), so each gets its own keyframes and easing. Do NOT try to shape both edges in a single keyframe list; per-keyframe `animation-timing-function` applies to every property in that keyframe.
- `--progress-bar-animation` applies in both states and can be used for effects such as a determinate shimmer. `revert-rule` is used to only override when it is set, to maximize composability.
- Never disable the indeterminate animation (`animation: none` leaves a zero-width fill in Chromium). For reduced motion, slow it down instead.

```css
progress {
  /* Lets the fill position itself in track units (cqi) */
  container-type: inline-size;
  /* Segment length relative to the track */
  --progress-bar-indeterminate-size: 30%;
  --progress-bar-indeterminate-animation-duration: 1.5s;

  /* Respect prefers-reduced-motion */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    --progress-bar-indeterminate-animation-duration: 10s;
  }
}

progress:indeterminate {
  --progress-bar-animation: var(
      --progress-bar-indeterminate-animation,
      progress-bar-indeterminate var(--progress-bar-indeterminate-animation-duration) infinite linear
    );
}

/* Add to the existing pseudo-element rules */
progress::-webkit-progress-value {
  animation: var(--progress-bar-animation, revert-rule);
}

progress::-moz-progress-bar {
  animation: var(--progress-bar-animation, revert-rule);
}

/* Physical: left to right regardless of direction (reverse under :dir(rtl) to mirror). */
@keyframes progress-bar-indeterminate {
  from, to {
    inline-size: var(--progress-bar-indeterminate-size);
    /* Pin to the physical left; Chromium would otherwise lay the fill out at the inline start */
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  from { translate: -100%; } /* Own width: fully outside the left edge */
  to { translate: 100cqi; }  /* Track width: fully outside the right edge */
}
```

### Additional Accessibility Considerations

In addition to the guidelines mentioned above:
* **Announce completion without stealing focus**: Do not set `tabindex` or call `.focus()` on `<progress>`, as it is non-interactive and moving focus to it disrupts keyboard users. To announce when a background task finishes, update a polite status element (`role="status"` or `aria-live="polite"`).
* **Do not add fallback text inside `<progress>`**: Text inside `<progress>` is ignored by modern browsers and is not used by assistive technologies.
* Avoid `aria-busy` + `aria-describedby` on an unlabeled generic `<div>`. `aria-busy` has inconsistent screen reader support, and `aria-describedby` on an unnamed `<div>` without a role is ignored by assistive technologies. If a region's content is not ready while loading, hide it with `hidden` or `aria-hidden="true"`.
* **DO** ensure proper contrast between the progress bar and track when adding custom styles.
* **Consider alternatives to represent indeterminate state**: Indeterminate progress bar animations displayed in parallel to other content can be considered distracting for some users.

## Fallback strategies

### Fallbacks & browser support for accent-color

Browser support for accent-color: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 93 (Aug 2021), Edge 93 (Sep 2021), Firefox 92 (Sep 2021), and Safari 26.2 (Dec 2025).

The `accent-color` property is progressive enhancement.
Browsers that do not support this property will ignore it and use their default UI colors.

If `accent-color` is not supported and styling the progress bar is required, use the customized styling method to apply preferred styling to the progress bar.

### Fallbacks & browser support for `revert-rule`

Baseline status for revert-rule: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2026-09-14.
Supported by: Chrome 148 (May 2026), Edge 148 (May 2026), Firefox 150 (Apr 2026), and Safari 27 (Sep 2026).

If `revert-rule` is not supported, `animation: var(--progress-bar-animation, revert-rule)` falls back to `animation-name: revert-rule` with a `0s` duration when `--progress-bar-animation` is unset, which is a harmless no-op when no `@keyframes revert-rule` exists.

# Build a Scrollspy Navigation

A scrollspy navigation is a common UI pattern that automatically highlights the navigation link corresponding to the section of the page currently in the viewport. This provides users with a clear sense of where they are in a long document as they scroll.

This pattern is common in two scenarios:
- **Single-page websites**: Where the primary site navigation links to different sections of a single page rather than separate URLs.
- **Long-form content**: Where a table of contents or sidebar navigation tracks the user's progress through an article, guide, or documentation.

## How to implement

Traditionally, the scrollspy pattern required JavaScript and the `IntersectionObserver` API. Modern CSS provides a native way to achieve this using the `scroll-target-group` property and the `:target-current` pseudo-class.

1.  **Ensure Navigation Links use Fragment Identifiers**: Your navigation must consist of anchor links (`<a>`) that point to sections on the same page using fragment identifiers (e.g., `<a href="#section-1">`) that match the `id` of your content sections.
2.  **Enable Scroll Target Grouping**: Apply the `scroll-target-group: auto` property to the container of your navigation links. This tells the browser to treat these links as markers that track their respective targets.
3.  **Style the Active Link**: Use the `:target-current` pseudo-class on your navigation links to apply styles when their target section is visible in the scroll container.
4.  **Semantic State**: Use a script to update `aria-current="true"` on the active link when scrolling stops to ensure the state is correctly communicated to assistive technologies.

## Example code

This example highlights the active link in a fixed navigation menu as the user scrolls through sections.

```css

/* MANDATORY: The container of markers must have scroll-target-group: auto */
nav {
  /* Enables automatic tracking of fragment links */
  scroll-target-group: auto;
}
/* MANDATORY: Use :target-current to style the link whose target is in view. */
nav a:target-current {
  color: var(--accent, #007bff);
  border-bottom-color: var(--accent, #007bff);
}
```

```html
<nav>
  <a href="#intro">Introduction</a>
  <a href="#features">Features</a>
  <a href="#pricing">Pricing</a>
</nav>

<main>
  <!-- MANDATORY: Each section must have a unique ID matching a link fragment in the <nav> and tabindex="-1" so it can receive focus. -->
  <section id="intro" tabindex="-1">
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <!-- content -->
  </section>
  <section id="features" tabindex="-1">
    <h2>Features</h2>
    <!-- content -->
  </section>
  <section id="pricing" tabindex="-1">
    <h2>Pricing</h2>
    <!-- content -->
  </section>
</main>

<script type="module">
  if (CSS.supports('scroll-target-group: auto')) {
    const syncAriaCurrent = () => {
      const currentLink = document.querySelector('nav a:target-current');
      document.querySelectorAll('nav a').forEach(link => {
        link.setAttribute('aria-current', link === currentLink ? 'true' : 'false');
      });
    };
    // Update the aria on initial page load. Ensure the DOM content is loaded, either by using `<script type="module">` or an event listener for `DOMContentLoaded`.
    syncAriaCurrent();
    // Update the aria after scroll ends
    document.addEventListener('scrollend', syncAriaCurrent);
  }
</script>
```

## Accessibility considerations

To ensure that people can navigate the scrollable region after selecting an anchor element, apply the corresponding element with a `tabindex="-1"` attribute. This will allow the element to receive focus, and allow people using a keyboard to scroll the container, or easily navigate from that content.

```html
<li><a href="#section-2">Section 2</a></li>
…
<!-- Adding a tabindex="-1" attribute to the section target will mean that it can receive focus after being navigated to -->
<section id="section-2" tabindex="-1">
  <h2>Section 2</h2>
</section>
```

To ensure consistent operability across different browsers and assistive technology combinations, if the scrollable container is not the `<body>` element, ensure that it can receive focus (by applying `tabindex="0"`), and has an accessible name that describes the content, along with an appropriate role (such as `role="region"`), if it does not already have an implicit role (which elements like `<main>` do).

## Best Practices

- **DO** use `scroll-target-group: auto` on a dedicated container for navigation links: This ensures the browser knows which links should be tracked.
- **DO** ensure targets have unique `id` attributes matching the links' `href` attributes.
- **DO** provide enough vertical space for sections: `scroll-target-group` most clearly identifies the visible target when sections are large enough that only one or two are visible at a time.
- **MANDATORY**: For accessibility, the visual state must be mirrored with `aria-current`. While `:target-current` handles the visual aspect, this is not exposed to screen readers.
- **AVOID** relying solely on color for the active state: Include other visual cues like font weight, an underline, or an indicator dot, or ensure there is sufficient contrast between the colors to ensure the difference is accessible to users with color vision deficiencies.

## Fallback strategies

Browser support for scroll-target-group: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 140 (Sep 2025) and Edge 140 (Sep 2025).
Unsupported in: Firefox and Safari.

For browsers that do not support `scroll-target-group`, you should use a fallback based on the `IntersectionObserver` API.

```html
<script>
  // Feature detection for scroll-target-group
  const supportsScrollTargetGroup = CSS.supports('scroll-target-group: auto');

  if (!supportsScrollTargetGroup) {
    const navLinks = document.querySelectorAll('nav a');
    const sections = document.querySelectorAll('main section');

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      entries.forEach(entry => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          const id = entry.target.getAttribute('id');
          navLinks.forEach(link => {
            const isActive = link.getAttribute('href') === `#${id}`;
            // Use the escaped colon class for consistent styling
            link.classList.toggle('target-current', isActive);
            link.setAttribute('aria-current', isActive);
          });
        }
      });
    }, { rootMargin: '-50% 0px -50% 0px', threshold: 0 });

    sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* Fallback style if :target-current is not supported.
     Use :is() to combine the native pseudo-class with a fallback class for older browsers. DO NOT use `nav a:target-current, nav a.target-current, as the unrecognized pseudoselector invalidates the entire rule in older browsers. */
  nav a:is(:target-current, .target-current) {
    color: var(--accent, #007bff);
    border-bottom-color: var(--accent, #007bff);
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>
```

# Loading spinner

A loading spinner (or activity indicator) informs users that a process is underway when the exact duration is unknown. Unlike a progress ring, a spinner is "indeterminate" and typically uses animation to signal activity.

This guide implements a spinner by:

- Using the native `<progress>` element as the semantic foundation. By omitting the `value` attribute, the browser treats it as an indeterminate progress bar, ensuring correct announcement by assistive technologies.
- Styling the component with `conic-gradient()` to create a visual "trail" and `background-clip: border-area` to hollow out the center into a ring.
- Animating the spinner efficiently using CSS transforms and respecting `prefers-reduced-motion` to ensure a comfortable experience for all users.

See `progress-ring` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "progress-ring"`) for handling determinate tasks with a known duration.


## Implementation

### 1. Markup

Use the native `<progress>` element as both the semantic source of truth and the visual component. Without a `value` attribute, it is implicitly indeterminate.

```html
<progress aria-label="Loading" class="loading-spinner"></progress>
```

### 2. Styles

#### Hiding Native UI
To style the `<progress>` element as a spinner, first hide the default browser styling for indeterminate progress bars.

```css
/* Hide native bars */
progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate::-webkit-progress-bar {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate::-webkit-progress-value {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate::-moz-progress-bar {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate::slider-fill {
  display: none;
  background: none;
}
```

#### Spinner Ring and Trail
The spinner uses a `conic-gradient` to create a visual trail. Forced colors mode removes background gradients, so in `@media (forced-colors: active)`, opt the spinner out with `forced-color-adjust: none` and draw it with system colors, or the spinner disappears.

```css
@property --arc-start {
  syntax: "<angle>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 0deg;
}
@property --arc-end {
  syntax: "<angle>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 0deg;
}
/* Use a custom property to conditionally include the dash animation */
@property --progress-dash-animation {
  syntax: "*";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: , progress-dash 3s ease-in-out infinite;
}

progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate {
  --_from: calc(90deg + var(--arc-start, 0deg));
  --_to: calc(90deg + var(--arc-end, 158deg));
  --size: 40px;
  --thickness: 2px;
  --spinner-color: #2563eb;
  --track-color: #e2e5e7;
  --spinner-duration: 1.5s;
  --_used-spinner-duration: var(--spinner-duration);
  --spinner-timing: linear;

  position: relative;
  width: var(--size);
  height: var(--size);
  border-radius: 50%;
  appearance: none;

  /* Create the fading trail with dynamic angles */
  background: conic-gradient(
      from var(--_from),
      var(--spinner-color) calc(var(--_to) - var(--_from)),
      transparent 0
    )
    var(--track-color);

  @supports (background-clip: border-area) {
    background-clip: border-area;
    border: var(--thickness) solid transparent;
    background-origin: border-box;
  }

  /* ... fallback for background-clip: border-area ... */

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    /* Keeps the gradient, which forced colors mode removes, and draws it in system colors */
    forced-color-adjust: none;
    --spinner-color: CanvasText;
    --track-color: Canvas;
  }

  /* The dash animation is only included if @property is supported */
  animation:
    progress-spin var(--_used-spinner-duration) linear infinite
    var(--progress-dash-animation, );
}

@keyframes progress-spin {
  to {
    rotate: 1turn;
  }
}

@keyframes progress-dash {
  from {
    --arc-start: 0deg;
    --arc-end: 3deg;
  }
  50% {
    --arc-start: 100deg;
    --arc-end: 358deg;
  }
  to {
    --arc-start: 360deg;
    --arc-end: 363deg;
  }
}
```

#### Respecting Motion Preferences

Users with motion sensitivities may find fast-spinning elements disorienting. Always respect the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query. Set the internal `--_used-spinner-duration` property to override the user's `--spinner-duration` value, using the same selector as the spinner rule so that the override applies, and drop the dash animation so that only a slow rotation remains.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  progress.loading-spinner:indeterminate {
    /* Slow down the animation significantly rather than stopping it entirely,
       so the user still knows that the process is active. */
    --_used-spinner-duration: 6s;
    /* Drops the dash animation, so the arc keeps a fixed length */
    --progress-dash-animation: ;
    --arc-end: 158deg;
  }
}
```

## Fallback strategies

Baseline status for Registered custom properties: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2024-07-09.
Supported by: Chrome 85 (Aug 2020), Edge 85 (Aug 2020), Firefox 128 (Jul 2024), and Safari 16.4 (Mar 2023).

If `@property` is supported, the dash animation is automatically included via the `--progress-dash-animation` property's `initial-value`. In browsers without `@property` support, the property registration is ignored, and the animation falls back to a simple rotation. No JavaScript is required for this fallback.

Browser support for background-clip: border-area: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 150 (Jun 2026), Edge 150 (Jul 2026), and Safari 18.2 (Dec 2024).
Unsupported in: Firefox.

For browsers that don't yet support `background-clip: border-area`, fall back to a `mask-image` to hollow out the center.

```css
/* Fallback: use mask-image to create the ring */
@supports not (background-clip: border-area) {
  --clip-boundary: calc(100% - var(--thickness));
  mask-image: radial-gradient(
    farthest-side,
    transparent var(--clip-boundary),
    black var(--clip-boundary)
  );
  border: 0;
}
```

# Stack Drill Down

## Overview

A stack drill-down is a hierarchical navigation pattern, common in mobile apps, where activating a link pushes a new full-screen view on top of the previous one. The view's content is application-defined — a settings sub-page, a thread inside a feed, a folder inside a file browser, a detail page inside a gallery, etc. The user returns by swiping the current view off-screen to the right or by tapping a back button. Browser history stays in sync so the OS-level Back gesture, deep links, and forward/back navigation all work coherently.

This guide implements the stack as:

- A horizontal CSS scroll-snap container where each view is exactly one snap stop. Drilling down appends a new view and smooth-scrolls to it; the swipe-back gesture is handled natively by the browser, giving momentum, velocity, and interruption tracking for free with no pointer-event JavaScript.
- A `scrollsnapchange` event listener on the stack that fires when the snap target changes. This is used as the single source of truth for "the active view changed" — so swipe, click, programmatic scroll, and `popstate` paths all converge in one callback that updates `inert`, restores focus, prunes views the user swiped past, and reconciles browser history.
- A `pushState` / `popstate` integration so every drill-down adds a history entry, the OS-level Back gesture works, and deep links open directly into the right view.
- An (optional) scroll-driven `view()` animation on each view that produces a parallax + dim + shadow effect tied directly to the swipe gesture, so the visible motion is driven by the user's finger, not a tween.

This approach is preferred over JavaScript-driven `transform` animations because the snap mechanism gives the user direct gestural control of the panel's position (their finger drives it, not a tween) and matches the interaction patterns users expect from native mobile apps.

## Implementation

### 1. Markup

The static HTML is just an empty stack container; views are built in JavaScript and appended as the user navigates.

```html
<div class="Stack">
  <!-- Intentionally empty. The initial view is appended by JavaScript
       at init time (step 8). -->
</div>
```

Each view is a `.Stack-view` direct child of `.Stack` (the snap target) with a `.Stack-viewContent` wrapper inside it (where view content lives, and where the parallax transform applies — see step 2). At any moment the stack has one view per active history entry, left-to-right in drill-down order. After the user has drilled in two levels from the root the rendered DOM looks like this:

```html
<div class="Stack">
  <!-- Root view. Has whatever content makes sense as the entry point
       of this section of the app. No back button — there is nothing
       behind the root in the stack. -->
  <div class="Stack-view" inert>
    <!-- tabindex="-1" lets focus move to the root's content when there is
         no link or back button to focus (step 7). -->
    <div class="Stack-viewContent" tabindex="-1">
      <!-- Root content; includes <a href> links that drill in. -->
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- First-level drill-down view. The user got here by activating a
       link in the root view. -->
  <div class="Stack-view" inert>
    <div class="Stack-viewContent">
      <header>
        <!-- DO include a back button. The swipe gesture is not
             discoverable or available to all users, so keyboard and
             pointer users need an explicit control. Name it after the
             view it returns to (step 7). -->
        <button class="back" aria-label="Back to Inbox"></button>
        <h2>Message</h2>
      </header>
      <main>
        <!-- View content; may include further drill-down <a href> links. -->
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Second-level drill-down view. Currently visible — no `inert`
       attribute. Same shape as the first-level view. -->
  <div class="Stack-view">
    <div class="Stack-viewContent">
      <header>
        <button class="back" aria-label="Back to Message"></button>
        <h2><!-- ... --></h2>
      </header>
      <main><!-- ... --></main>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Notes:

- All views except the currently-visible one carry the `inert` attribute. This is applied/removed automatically by the `scrollsnapchange` handler in step 7 — do not set it from your view builders.
- Views the user swipes back past are removed from the DOM (also by step 7) so the stack never grows beyond `currentDepth + 1` children. They are rebuilt on demand from their cached URL paths if forward navigation returns to them.

### 2. Styles

#### The stack scroller

The stack is a horizontal grid where each child view is exactly the width of the container, with CSS scroll-snap enforcing one-view-per-snap. This is what gives the swipe-back gesture its native feel.

```css
.Stack {
  /* Use dvh so the height tracks the dynamic viewport on mobile, where
     the address bar can show/hide. svh would clip during the address bar
     animation; vh leaks under it. */
  height: 100dvh;

  /* Lay views out left-to-right, each one full-width, so horizontal
     scrolling moves between them one at a time. */
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  grid-auto-columns: 100%;
  grid-template-rows: 100%;
  overflow-x: auto;

  /* `mandatory` guarantees the stack always settles fully on a view —
     never half-way between two. */
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  /* Prevent the swipe-back gesture from chaining into the browser's
     own history-back gesture (iOS, some Android) or the page's vertical
     scroll. The user is navigating the stack, not the page. */
  overscroll-behavior-x: none;
}

/* Hide the visual scrollbar — the snap and the parallax are the
   affordances; a horizontal scrollbar would look out of place. */
.Stack::-webkit-scrollbar {
  display: none;
}

/* MANDATORY: Opt into smooth programmatic scrolling via CSS, gated on
   prefers-reduced-motion. JS code calls scrollTo/scrollBy with
   behavior: 'auto' which defers to this rule, so the OS-level reduced-
   motion preference automatically downgrades to instant scrolling
   without any per-call JS branching. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .Stack {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }
}

.Stack-view {
  scroll-snap-align: start;
  /* `always` prevents the user from blowing through more than one view
     per gesture, so depth changes always happen one step at a time. */
  scroll-snap-stop: always;
}

/* MANDATORY: A separate inner element is required for the parallax
   transform below. Applying transforms directly to the snap target
   (.Stack-view) would feed back into the scroll container's snap
   geometry and the scroller would jump mid-gesture. */
.Stack-viewContent {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  /* Set the text color with the background color so the pair keeps its
     contrast when the page uses a dark color scheme. */
  background-color: Canvas;
  color: CanvasText;
  /* Each view scrolls its own content vertically, independent of the
     stack's horizontal scroll. */
  overflow-y: auto;
}
```

#### The "stack" effect (parallax / dim / shadow)

A scroll-driven `view(inline)` animation tracks each view's progress through the stack scroller and applies a parallax + dim to the exiting view, plus a shadow on incoming drill-down views so they read as "cards" stacking over the previous view.

```css
/* MANDATORY: Wrap the animation block in @supports. Browsers without
   scroll-driven animations still parse the @keyframes and would
   apply the `to` state as a static style, leaving every view
   permanently transformed. The @supports gate confines the animation
   to browsers where it actually animates. */
@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
  .Stack-viewContent {
    /* view(inline) tracks this element's progress through its nearest
       scrollable ancestor on the inline (x) axis. */
    animation: parallax linear both;
    animation-timeline: view(inline);
    /* Only animate the EXIT phase — when this view is being covered
       by a deeper one. During its own entry the view stays at rest,
       so the fresh content is fully bright and in position throughout. */
    animation-range: exit 0% exit 100%;
  }

  /* Drill-down views (everything except the root) also get a shadow on
     their left edge during the transition so they feel like cards
     stacking over the previous view. */
  .Stack-view:not(:first-child) .Stack-viewContent {
    animation: parallax linear both, shadow-fade linear both;
    animation-timeline: view(inline), view(inline);
    /* parallax: only exit (the view sliding back as a deeper one comes in).
       shadow-fade: entry through exit (visible the whole time the view is
       transitioning, not when it's at rest). */
    animation-range: exit 0% exit 100%, entry 0% exit 100%;
  }

  @keyframes parallax {
    /* translateX(75%) and brightness(0.8) are examples — adjust to taste. */
    to {
      transform: translateX(75%);
      filter: brightness(0.8);
    }
  }

  @keyframes shadow-fade {
    /* Shadow ramps in during entry, holds across the middle of the
       gesture, and ramps out during exit — so it's only visible while
       the view is mid-transition, not when at rest. */
    0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 1.5rem #0000; }
    25%, 75% { box-shadow: 0 0 1.5rem #0004; }
  }
}
```

NOTE: This effect is popular in modern native stack applications and is a good starting point, but the exact visual effects can be customized to fit existing transition styles as needed.

### 3. Module state

The stack tracks four pieces of state in module scope:

```js
const stack = document.querySelector('.Stack');

// Reference to the root view DOM element. The Stack starts empty, so
// this is null until the root view is created — either at init (step 8,
// when the URL is '/') or later by synthesizeRootEntry() (when the user
// landed on a deep link and then navigates back). Held as a mutable
// reference because other code (the scrollsnapchange handler, init, etc.)
// uses identity comparisons against it.
let rootView = null;

// Tracks which element to restore focus to when the user swipes back
// into a previous view. Keyed by the view element itself so entries
// are garbage-collected automatically when the view is pruned.
const returnFocus = new WeakMap();

// Maps history depth -> {urlPath, view}. We MUST maintain this map
// ourselves because the History API does not expose state for entries
// other than the current one — so when a view is pruned on swipe-back,
// we still need to remember which URL it represented in case the user
// later forward-navigates back into it.
const entriesByDepth = new Map();

// Tracked manually because history.state on a popstate event tells us
// the destination depth but not where we came from. We need both to
// compute the direction (back vs forward) and the distance.
let currentDepth = 0;
```

Plus three application-specific helpers — the only places where your app's routing and view rendering plug in:

```js
// Resolve a URL path to the data your app needs to render the
// corresponding drill-down view, or return null for paths this section
// of the app does not handle (the root path '/', external links,
// unknown routes). resolveUrl() is for drill-down routes only — the
// root view is rendered separately by createRootView() below.
function resolveUrl(urlPath) {
  // Replace with your routing logic. For example, match `/view/:id`
  // and look the id up in your app state.
}

// Build the root (home) view of the stack. Application-specific content;
// preserve the .Stack-view / .Stack-viewContent wrapper structure (the
// inner element is required for the parallax — see step 2) and DO NOT
// render a back button — the root view has nothing behind it in the
// stack.
function createRootView() {
  const view = document.createElement('div');
  view.className = 'Stack-view';
  view.innerHTML = `
    <!-- tabindex="-1" lets step 7 focus the root's content when there is
         no link to return focus to. -->
    <div class="Stack-viewContent" tabindex="-1">
      <!-- Root content. Include <a href> elements pointing at URL paths
           that resolveUrl() accepts, to enable drill-down from here. -->
    </div>
  `;
  return view;
}

// Build a drill-down view DOM element from the resolved route data.
// Customize the inner content freely, but DO preserve the .Stack-view
// / .Stack-viewContent wrapper structure and DO include a back button
// (the swipe gesture is not discoverable or available to all users).
function createDrillDownView(routeData) {
  const view = document.createElement('div');
  view.className = 'Stack-view';
  view.innerHTML = `
    <div class="Stack-viewContent">
      <header>
        <!-- Step 7 names this button after the view it returns to. -->
        <button class="back" aria-label="Back"></button>
        <h2></h2>
        <!-- Breadcrumb or other view chrome derived from routeData. -->
      </header>
      <main>
        <!-- View body, also from routeData. Include further <a href>
             elements pointing at URL paths that resolveUrl() also
             accepts, to enable additional drill-downs from this view. -->
      </main>
    </div>
  `;
  // Set the title as text so it can't inject markup. (`title` is an
  // example field; use whatever your routeData provides.)
  view.querySelector('h2').textContent = routeData.title;
  return view;
}

function getCurrentUrlPath() {
  return location.pathname;
}
```

### 4. Drill down

A drill-down does four things in this order: push a history entry, build the new view, append it, smooth-scroll to it. The `scrollsnapchange` handler (step 7) picks up from there once the snap settles.

```js
function drillDown(urlPath) {
  const routeData = resolveUrl(urlPath);
  if (!routeData) return;

  const newDepth = currentDepth + 1;
  // Push BEFORE creating the view so the URL is correct if anything
  // observing history (analytics, etc.) reads it during view creation.
  history.pushState({depth: newDepth}, '', urlPath);

  // pushState truncates forward entries in real browser history;
  // mirror that truncation in our depth map so we don't hold references
  // to views the user can no longer reach.
  for (const d of entriesByDepth.keys()) {
    if (d >= newDepth) entriesByDepth.delete(d);
  }
  currentDepth = newDepth;

  const newView = createDrillDownView(routeData);
  stack.appendChild(newView);
  entriesByDepth.set(newDepth, {urlPath, view: newView});

  // Scroll one viewport-width to the right. behavior: 'auto' defers to
  // the CSS `scroll-behavior` set in step 2, which is smooth unless
  // prefers-reduced-motion is set. The snap container locks onto the
  // new view; the scrollsnapchange listener (step 7) fires when the
  // snap settles.
  stack.scrollBy({left: stack.clientWidth, behavior: 'auto'});
}
```

### 5. Click and back-button handling

Intercept link clicks inside the stack and convert them to drill-downs. Preserve modifier-key behavior so cmd/middle-click still opens the link in a new tab.

```js
stack.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // Back button: defer to goBack() (defined below), which handles both
  // the normal in-app case and the deep-link case.
  if (e.target.closest('.back')) {
    goBack();
    return;
  }

  // Drill-down link.
  const link = e.target.closest('a');
  if (!link || !stack.contains(link)) return;
  // Let the browser handle modified clicks so users can open links
  // in new tabs / windows. e.button !== 0 filters out middle-clicks.
  if (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.shiftKey || e.button !== 0) return;

  const urlPath = new URL(link.href).pathname;
  const parentView = link.closest('.Stack-view');
  // If the URL isn't handled by this section of the app (resolveUrl
  // returns null), fall through so the browser navigates normally.
  if (!resolveUrl(urlPath) || !parentView) return;

  e.preventDefault();
  // Record which link the user activated so focus can be restored to
  // it when they swipe (or click) back into this view.
  returnFocus.set(parentView, link);
  drillDown(urlPath);
});

// Going back is usually just history.back(), but there's an important
// edge case: when the user lands directly on a deep-linked URL, there
// is no in-app history entry behind it. Calling history.back() in that
// situation would take them out of the app entirely. MANDATORY: detect
// this case and synthesize a root entry instead, so an in-app Back from
// a deep link lands on the root view and the platform Back from there
// returns the user to where they came from.
function goBack() {
  const atDeepLinkRoot = currentDepth === 0
    && entriesByDepth.get(0)?.view !== rootView;
  if (atDeepLinkRoot) {
    synthesizeRootEntry();
  } else {
    // history.back() fires popstate, which routes through
    // updateFromHistoryState (step 6) and scrolls the stack — the
    // same path a swipe-back converges on.
    history.back();
  }
}

function synthesizeRootEntry() {
  // Push a new history entry pointing at the root URL. This becomes the
  // entry the user "came from"; the original deep-linked entry is now
  // behind us, so platform Back from the root view will return there.
  const newDepth = currentDepth + 1;
  history.pushState({depth: newDepth}, '', '/');

  // Create the root view if it doesn't exist yet (we landed on a deep
  // link and never needed it before now), and insert it at the LEFT end
  // of the stack. Adjust scrollLeft by one viewport width so the user's
  // view doesn't visually jump — they should still be looking at the
  // deep-linked view until the scroll animation below runs.
  if (!rootView) {
    rootView = createRootView();
    stack.prepend(rootView);
    stack.scrollLeft += stack.clientWidth;
  }
  entriesByDepth.set(newDepth, {urlPath: '/', view: rootView});

  // Now scroll to the new entry (the root view). updateFromHistoryState
  // smooth-scrolls one step left, the parallax plays, and
  // scrollsnapchange fires when the root view settles.
  updateFromHistoryState(history.state);
}
```

### 6. Sync from history (popstate)

`popstate` fires when the user uses the browser/OS back or forward button, or when JavaScript calls `history.back()` / `.go()`. This handler is the only path that scrolls the stack in response to a history change.

```js
window.addEventListener('popstate', (event) => {
  updateFromHistoryState(event.state);
});

function updateFromHistoryState(state, behaviorOverride) {
  const newDepth = state?.depth ?? 0;
  const urlPath = getCurrentUrlPath();

  // Ensure entriesByDepth has an entry for the destination depth.
  // If the URL changed (e.g. forward-nav into a previously-pruned
  // view), clear the cached view reference so the loop below rebuilds.
  const entry = entriesByDepth.get(newDepth) ?? {view: null};
  if (entry.urlPath !== urlPath) {
    entry.urlPath = urlPath;
    entry.view = urlPath === '/' ? rootView : null;
  }
  entriesByDepth.set(newDepth, entry);

  // Rebuild any views between root and the destination that were
  // pruned earlier (when the user swiped back past them). Without
  // this, forward-navigating to a previously-pruned view would have
  // no element to scroll to.
  for (let d = 0; d <= newDepth; d++) {
    const e = entriesByDepth.get(d);
    if (!e || e.view) continue;
    const routeData = resolveUrl(e.urlPath);
    if (!routeData) continue;
    const rebuilt = createDrillDownView(routeData);
    stack.appendChild(rebuilt);
    e.view = rebuilt;
  }

  currentDepth = newDepth;

  const targetView = entriesByDepth.get(newDepth)?.view;
  if (!targetView) return;

  // Compare destination index against current scroll position so we
  // can bail if they're already aligned. This is reached when the
  // scrollsnapchange handler below calls history.go() to sync history
  // after a swipe-back that already completed visually — there's
  // nothing more to scroll.
  const toIdx = [...stack.children].indexOf(targetView);
  const fromIdx = Math.round(stack.scrollLeft / stack.clientWidth);
  if (fromIdx === toIdx) return;

  // Pick a scroll behavior:
  //   - multi-step jumps (e.g. history.go(-3)): 'instant' to skip
  //     intermediate snap points — otherwise smooth-scrolling would
  //     fire scrollsnapchange for each one and do N rounds of
  //     state-transition work for no reason.
  //   - rightward (forward) single-step: 'instant'. Browser-forward is
  //     rare on the web and is often spurious (e.g. iOS Safari treats
  //     edge swipes as forward navigation, even with overscroll-behavior
  //     set). An instant swap reads as "snap" rather than a misleading
  //     drilldown animation the user didn't ask for. The user-initiated
  //     drill-down path (drillDown, step 4) is unaffected — it calls
  //     scrollBy directly and never reaches this code.
  //   - leftward (back) single-step: 'auto' so the CSS `scroll-behavior`
  //     (smooth unless prefers-reduced-motion is set — see step 2)
  //     applies. Back is the common, expected case and benefits from
  //     the animation.
  // NOTE: "forward" here means spatial direction (toIdx > fromIdx),
  // NOT depth direction. synthesizeRootEntry (step 5) pushes a new
  // depth but scrolls LEFT to the root view, which correctly reads as
  // back-style (smooth).
  const forward = toIdx > fromIdx;
  const multiStep = Math.abs(toIdx - fromIdx) > 1;
  const behavior = behaviorOverride ?? (forward || multiStep ? 'instant' : 'auto');
  stack.scrollTo({left: toIdx * stack.clientWidth, behavior});
}
```

### 7. `scrollsnapchange`: the single source of truth

After every snap commit — whether triggered by a swipe, a click, or a programmatic scroll — the browser fires a `scrollsnapchange` event on the scroll container, with the newly snapped element exposed as `event.snapTargetInline` (for horizontal snapping). Putting all state transitions inside this one handler is what keeps the swipe path, the click path, and the `popstate` path coherent.

The handler is extracted into a standalone `onActiveViewChanged` function so the fallback (see "Fallback strategies" below) can reuse it without duplicating the logic.

```js
function onActiveViewChanged(currentView) {
  // Note whether focus is in the stack before views are made inert or
  // removed, so the focus fallback below only moves focus that would
  // otherwise be lost, and never moves it on page load.
  const focusWasInStack = stack.contains(document.activeElement);

  // Walk the stack in DOM order to update each view's role:
  //  - Views at or before currentView stay in the DOM but get
  //    `inert` (except currentView) so focus, pointer events, and
  //    AT navigation cannot leak into views hidden behind the
  //    parallax.
  //  - Views after currentView are unreachable (the user swiped
  //    back past them) so we drop them from the DOM to free memory.
  //    Their urlPath stays in entriesByDepth so a later forward
  //    navigation can rebuild the view from scratch.
  let seenCurrent = false;
  for (const view of [...stack.children]) {
    if (seenCurrent) {
      for (const e of entriesByDepth.values()) {
        if (e.view === view) e.view = null;
      }
      view.remove();
    } else {
      // MANDATORY: inert non-current views. Without this, tabbing
      // and screen-reader navigation can reach content hidden behind
      // the parallax — a severe accessibility failure that's
      // invisible to sighted users.
      view.toggleAttribute('inert', view !== currentView);
      if (view === currentView) seenCurrent = true;
    }
  }

  // If the visible view's depth doesn't match `currentDepth`, the
  // user got here by swiping (not clicking) — sync history so the
  // browser back/forward buttons stay coherent with what's on screen.
  let currentViewDepth;
  for (const [d, e] of entriesByDepth) {
    if (e.view === currentView) currentViewDepth = d;
  }
  if (currentViewDepth !== undefined && currentViewDepth !== currentDepth) {
    // history.go fires popstate, which re-enters updateFromHistoryState.
    // That call's fromIdx === toIdx check bails out without scrolling.
    history.go(currentViewDepth - currentDepth);
  }

  // Name the active view in the title, and use the app name alone for the
  // root. Each drill-down adds a history entry, and the title is what the
  // history list, the tab and screen readers use to tell them apart.
  // ('Mail' is an example app name.)
  const viewTitle = currentView.querySelector('h2')?.textContent;
  document.title = viewTitle ? `${viewTitle} | Mail` : 'Mail';

  // Name the back button after the view it returns to: the previous view
  // in the stack, or the root view after a deep link. Focus lands on it
  // after a drill-down, so "Back" alone tells screen reader users nothing
  // about where they are. ('Inbox' is the example root title.)
  const back = currentView.querySelector('.back');
  if (back) {
    const parentTitle =
      currentView.previousElementSibling?.querySelector('h2')?.textContent ?? 'Inbox';
    back.setAttribute('aria-label', `Back to ${parentTitle}`);
  }

  // Restore focus on the now-active view:
  //  - If we recorded which link the user activated to drill out
  //    of this view, return focus there so a swipe-back lands them
  //    exactly where they left off.
  //  - Otherwise (a freshly-pushed drill-down view), move focus to
  //    the back button so keyboard users have an obvious next action.
  //  - preventScroll is REQUIRED: without it, .focus() scrolls the
  //    snap container to bring the focused element into view, which
  //    fights the snap and can land the user mid-snap.
  const stored = returnFocus.get(currentView);
  if (stored?.isConnected) {
    stored.focus({preventScroll: true});
    returnFocus.delete(currentView);
  } else if (currentView !== rootView) {
    currentView.querySelector('.back')?.focus({preventScroll: true});
  } else if (focusWasInStack && !currentView.contains(document.activeElement)) {
    // The root has no back button, and after a deep link or a pruned view
    // there is no stored link either, so focus the root's content. Otherwise
    // focus would stay in a removed or inert view and fall to <body>, and
    // keyboard and screen reader users would restart from the top of the page.
    currentView.querySelector('.Stack-viewContent')?.focus({preventScroll: true});
  }
}

stack.addEventListener('scrollsnapchange', (event) => {
  // snapTargetInline is the element that was just snapped to on the
  // inline (horizontal) axis. For this stack — where each view is one
  // horizontal snap stop — that's the new active view.
  onActiveViewChanged(event.snapTargetInline);
});
```

### 8. Initialization (including deep links)

When the page loads, the URL may already point at a deep view (a shared link, a bookmark, a refresh on a deep page). Build whichever initial view matches the URL — root or deep-linked, but never both — append it to the empty stack, seed the depth-0 history entry, and run an initial scroll pass with `behavior: 'instant'` so the parallax doesn't animate on first paint.

```js
const initialUrlPath = getCurrentUrlPath();
const initialRouteData = resolveUrl(initialUrlPath);

// Build the initial view: a drill-down view if the URL maps to one,
// otherwise the root view. Whichever it is, that's the only view in
// the stack right now — the other will be created lazily by
// synthesizeRootEntry (step 5) or drillDown (step 4) if the user
// navigates to it.
let initialView;
if (initialRouteData) {
  initialView = createDrillDownView(initialRouteData);
} else {
  rootView = createRootView();
  initialView = rootView;
}
stack.appendChild(initialView);

entriesByDepth.set(0, {urlPath: initialUrlPath, view: initialView});
// replaceState attaches a `depth` to the entry the user landed on, so
// any subsequent pushState / popstate has a base depth to count from.
history.replaceState({depth: 0}, '');

updateFromHistoryState(history.state, 'instant');
```

### Best practices

- **DO** use the `scrollsnapchange` event (with an `IntersectionObserver` fallback — see "Fallback strategies") as the source of truth for "the active view changed", not scroll-event coordinates. Snap commit is the only event that fires consistently across swipe, click, programmatic scroll, and `popstate` paths.
- **DO** apply transforms to a child of the snap target, never to the snap target itself. A transform on the snap target feeds back into the scroll container's snap geometry and the scroller will glitch mid-gesture.
- **DO** apply `inert` to every view except the currently visible one. Without this, focus and screen-reader navigation leak into views hidden behind the parallax — invisible to sighted users but a severe accessibility failure.
- **DO** push a history entry on every drill-down and handle `popstate` so the OS-level Back gesture and the browser back/forward buttons work. This is what makes the pattern feel like a native app.
- **DO** reconcile history from the active-view-changed handler when a swipe-back lands on a view whose depth doesn't match `currentDepth`. Without this, a subsequent OS Back returns the user somewhere unexpected because the browser's history cursor is out of sync with what's on screen.
- **DO** prune views the user swiped past from the DOM. A long drill-down session can otherwise accumulate dozens of detached subtrees. The cached URL path in `entriesByDepth` is enough to rebuild any view if forward navigation returns to it.
- **DO** make sure focus ends up inside the active view after every active-view change, including Back from a deep link and jumps to the root. If there is no stored link and no back button, focus the view's `.Stack-viewContent` (given `tabindex="-1"`). Focus left in a removed or `inert` view falls to `<body>`.
- **DO** update `document.title` to name the active view on every active-view change. Each drill-down adds a history entry, and the title is what the browser's history list, the tab and screen readers use to tell those entries apart.
- **DO** call `.focus({preventScroll: true})` when restoring focus inside the stack. The default `preventScroll: false` makes the browser scroll the focus target into view, which fights the snap container and can land the user mid-snap.
- **DO** preserve cmd/ctrl/middle-click on internal links so URLs remain shareable and openable in a new tab.
- **DO** use `behavior: 'instant'` for multi-step history jumps AND for spatial-forward popstate transitions (`toIdx > fromIdx`). Multi-step jumps would otherwise fire `scrollsnapchange` at every intermediate snap and do N rounds of inert/focus/history work. Forward popstates are often spurious — iOS Safari treats edge swipes as browser forward even with `overscroll-behavior-x: none` set, and an instant swap is much less misleading than animating a "drilldown" the user didn't initiate. The user-initiated drill-down path (`drillDown`) is unaffected because it scrolls directly, not via `popstate`.
- **DO** respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: declare `scroll-behavior: smooth` only inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)` and call `scrollTo` / `scrollBy` with `behavior: 'auto'` (not `'smooth'`) so the OS-level preference takes effect without per-call JS branching. Hard-coding `behavior: 'smooth'` bypasses the user's setting.
- **DO** render real `<a href>` elements as drill-down triggers, not `<button onclick>` or `<div>`. Real anchors get URL preview on hover, shareability, middle-click, screen-reader role, and SEO for free.
- **DO** include an explicit back button in every drill-down view. The swipe gesture is not discoverable or available to all users — keyboard, pointer, and desktop users need a visible affordance. Horizontal arrow keys and trackpad swipes can also scroll the stack.
- **DO NOT** call `history.pushState` from the `popstate` handler — that pushes *new* entries while the user is trying to go back and breaks the browser back button.
- **DO NOT** drive the parallax with a `scroll` event listener when scroll-driven animations are available. The CSS path runs on the compositor; a JS scroll listener runs on the main thread and will visibly drop frames during the gesture.
- **DO NOT** mutate views you removed from the DOM after a swipe-back. Treat `entriesByDepth` as the canonical record: a pruned entry has `view: null` and is rebuilt on demand in `updateFromHistoryState`.

### Fallback strategies

Baseline status for Scroll snap: Widely available. It's been Baseline since 2020-01-15.
Supported by: Chrome 69 (Sep 2018), Edge 79 (Jan 2020), Firefox 68 (Jul 2019), and Safari 11 (Sep 2017).

Baseline status for Intersection observer: Widely available. It's been Baseline since 2019-03-25.
Supported by: Chrome 58 (Apr 2017), Edge 16 (Oct 2017), Firefox 55 (Aug 2017), Safari 12.1 (Mar 2019), and Safari iOS 12.2 (Mar 2019).

Baseline status for inert: Widely available. It's been Baseline since 2023-04-11.
Supported by: Chrome 102 (May 2022), Edge 102 (May 2022), Firefox 112 (Apr 2023), and Safari 15.5 (May 2022).

The features that may require fallbacks are scroll-snap-events and scroll-driven-animations, both of which have robust fallback or progressive enhancement stories, and are safe to use for this use case:

#### Scroll snap events

Browser support for Scroll snap events: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 129 (Sep 2024) and Edge 129 (Sep 2024).
Unsupported in: Firefox and Safari.

The `scrollsnapchange` event is the cleanest way to detect "the active view changed" — one listener on the stack, fired exactly once per snap commit. In browsers without it, the same effect can be polyfilled with an `IntersectionObserver` watching each view for full visibility inside the stack. The fallback dispatches into the same `onActiveViewChanged` function the primary path uses, so all the state-transition logic stays in one place.

```js
// MANDATORY when supporting browsers that haven't shipped scroll-snap-events
// yet. Check `HTMLElement.prototype` (not `window` or `document`) — the
// event handler IDL attribute is added to the prototype when the feature
// is supported, regardless of whether any element has the handler set.
if (!('onscrollsnapchange' in HTMLElement.prototype)) {
  const viewObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    for (const entry of entries) {
      // threshold:1 only fires for fully-visible entries, but the
      // observer also emits a "leaving" entry per view that drops below
      // ratio 1. Filter to the entering side, which is the snap-commit
      // moment we're trying to detect.
      if (entry.intersectionRatio === 1) {
        onActiveViewChanged(entry.target);
      }
    }
  }, {root: stack, threshold: 1});

  // Auto-observe every .Stack-view as it's added to the stack, and stop
  // observing as it's removed. Using a MutationObserver lets the primary
  // code (drillDown, updateFromHistoryState, synthesizeRootEntry, init)
  // stay free of fallback wiring.
  new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
    for (const m of mutations) {
      for (const node of m.addedNodes) {
        if (node.classList?.contains('Stack-view')) viewObserver.observe(node);
      }
      for (const node of m.removedNodes) {
        if (node.classList?.contains('Stack-view')) viewObserver.unobserve(node);
      }
    }
  }).observe(stack, {childList: true});

  // Catch up to any views already in the stack at the time this code
  // runs (typically the initial view appended in step 8).
  for (const view of stack.children) viewObserver.observe(view);
}
```

#### Scroll-driven animations

Baseline status for Scroll-driven animations: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2026-10-27.
Supported by: Chrome 115 (Jul 2023), Edge 115 (Jul 2023), Firefox 159 (Oct 2026), and Safari 26 (Sep 2025).

The scroll-driven parallax / dim / shadow effect is a progressive enhancement on top of the navigation core. The CSS `@supports (animation-timeline: view())` gate (shown in step 2) confines the animation to supporting browsers; everywhere else the views simply cut between snap stops with no transition. The component is fully functional without the parallax — snap, history sync, focus management, and `inert` all still work.

If a parallax fallback is required for older baseline targets, attach a `scroll` listener to the stack and write a CSS custom property describing each view's progress through the scrollport, then drive `transform` and `filter` from that property:

```js
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline: view()')) {
  stack.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    const viewWidth = stack.clientWidth;
    for (const view of stack.children) {
      // Progress: 0 when this view is centered, 1 when it has fully
      // exited to the left. Matches the @keyframes mapping above.
      const offsetLeft = view.offsetLeft - stack.scrollLeft;
      const progress = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, -offsetLeft / viewWidth));
      const content = view.querySelector('.Stack-viewContent');
      content.style.transform = `translateX(${progress * 75}%)`;
      content.style.filter = `brightness(${1 - progress * 0.2})`;
    }
  });
}
```

# Tabbed interfaces

## Basic markup

Instead of manually implementing a roving tabindex or ARIA activedescendant pattern to meet the focus management requirement of the ARIA `tablist` role, we can use `focusgroup` which has a `tablist` behavior and automatically assigns the right roles (`tablist` on the container and `tab` on its focusable children).

```html
<div class="tabs">
  <div focusgroup="tablist" aria-label="Web platform technologies">
      <button type="button" id="html-tab" aria-selected="true" focusgroupstart>HTML</button>
      <button type="button" id="css-tab">CSS</button>
      <button type="button" id="js-tab">JavaScript</button>
  </div>
  <div role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="html-tab" tabindex="0"><!-- content for HTML tab --></div>
  <div role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="css-tab" hidden="until-found"><!-- content for CSS tab --></div>
  <div role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="js-tab" hidden="until-found"><!-- content for JavaScript tab --></div>
</div>
```

DO NOT use this or the WAI-ARIA tabs pattern for page navigation, even if it is intended to be presented as a tabbed interface.

Tablist:
- Prefer `<button>` for the tabs to avoid needing to reimplement button-like behavior. Add `type=button` to avoid unexpected submitter behavior if nested within a form.
- Use `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label` to give the tablist an accessible name.
- Use `aria-selected` to indicate which tab is currently selected.

Tab panels:
- Set `role=tabpanel` on the tab panels and use `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label` to give the panel an accessible name (e.g. `aria-labelledby` can reference the associated tab).
- Do not add `aria-controls` to the tabs: no screen reader uses it.
- Use `hidden="until-found"` to hide tab panels whose tab is not actively selected while still keeping them searchable by the browser’s find-in-page feature. Use `hidden` with no value to remove the content from the browser's search in-page feature as well.
- When using `hidden="until-found"`, use the `beforematch` event to sync the tab & tab panel selection state when find-in-page makes a different tab active.
- Set `tabindex=0` on the visible tab panel if it contains no focusable elements, and remove it from hidden panels.

- MANDATORY: Render the initial state in the HTML: `hidden` on every panel except the selected one, and `aria-selected="true"` on the selected tab. Otherwise every panel shows until script runs, overlapping when they share a grid cell.

## Keyboard interaction

When it comes to how tabs become selected, one can either make _selection follow focus_ or allow the user to move focus between tabs and perform _selection by activation_.

Default to _selection follows focus_.
It saves a keypress per tab, is more discoverable, and matches native radio groups.
Use selection by activation instead when showing a panel has a cost the user should opt into, since arrowing from the first tab to the fifth would pay it for every tab along the way:
- Showing the panel is not instant: content is fetched on demand, or rendering it is noticeably slow.
- Selecting a tab has side effects: it changes the URL or history, sends analytics, starts media, or saves state.
- The panel shifts the layout significantly (e.g. panels of very different heights push the rest of the page around).

With either pattern, focus entering the group must land on the selected item. By default it returns to the last focused item instead, which is wrong once selection can change without focus (find-in-page, script).
Use `nomemory` and move `focusgroupstart` to the selected tab:

```html
<div focusgroup="tablist nomemory" aria-label="Web platform technologies">
```

```js
for (const tab of tabs) {
  tab.ariaSelected = tab === selected;
  tab.toggleAttribute('focusgroupstart', tab === selected);
}
```

## Styling

### Indicating the selected tab

Style the selected tab from `aria-selected`, so the visual state can never disagree with the accessible one.
Do not apply any styling that changes the dimensions of the tab (e.g., `padding`, `border`, `margin`, `font-size`, `font-weight`, etc).

- MANDATORY: Do not indicate the selected tab with color alone (WCAG 1.4.1). Add a shape, such as an underline.
- Draw the underline with a border on a pseudo-element. DO NOT use `box-shadow` or a background: forced colors mode removes both, leaving no indicator.

```css
[role="tab"] {
  position: relative;
}

[aria-selected="true"] {
  color: var(--accent);

  /* Example: a 3px underline that takes no space */
  &::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: auto 0 0;
    border-block-end: 3px solid var(--accent);
  }
}
```

To animate the underline between tabs, move the pseudo-element to the tablist and tether it to the selected tab with anchor positioning. See `anchor-positioning-tab-underline` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "anchor-positioning-tab-underline"`) for details.


- DO NOT put padding, borders, margins, or backgrounds on the panel element itself. `hidden=until-found` does not use `display: none`: it only hides the panel's contents, so a hidden panel still renders its padding, border, margin, and background as an empty strip. Put these styles on a child wrapper inside the panel instead.
- `hidden=until-found` panels still participate in grid and flex layout, so `gap` adds space for every hidden panel. Place all panels in the same grid cell, or avoid `gap` between panels.

See `search-hidden-content` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "search-hidden-content"`) for more details.

## Handling tabs that don’t fit

Keep tabs on a single row. DO NOT wrap them onto multiple rows: wrapped rows make the tab order and the selected tab hard to follow.
When the tabs are wider than the available space, either scroll the tablist or move the tabs that don’t fit into a popup menu.
Optionally, also shrink the inactive tabs, so more tabs fit before either kicks in.

1. **Scrolling** keeps every tab in the tablist and in the keyboard order, and works with touch swiping. It needs no script beyond selection. Use it by default, and always on touch-first interfaces.
2. **An overflow menu** shows the names of all hidden tabs at once, in a list, without scrolling. Use it when there are a few primary tabs and a tail of less used ones, and users mostly use a pointer. It needs script to detect overflow.
3. **Shrinking inactive tabs** truncates their labels, so it suits tabs the user creates (e.g. open documents in an editor) better than a fixed set of tabs with short labels. Combine it with scrolling or an overflow menu.

### Strategy 1: scrolling

1. Make the tablist a horizontal scroll container: `display: flex`, `overflow-x: auto`, and `flex: none` on the tabs so they never shrink (unless combined with strategy 3).
2. Optional: snap to tab edges with `scroll-snap-type: inline proximity` on the tablist and `scroll-snap-align: start` on the tabs. Use `proximity`, not `mandatory`, so the user can still stop between tabs.
3. Hide the scrollbar and fade out each edge of the tablist that has more tabs beyond it, as described below. A fade suits a row of tabs better than a shadow, and works on any background. See `scrollability-affordance-hints` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "scrollability-affordance-hints"`) for more details on scrollability hints.
4. On every selection change, scroll the selected tab into view with `scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', inline: 'nearest', container: 'nearest' })`. `container: 'nearest'` scrolls only the tablist, not the page. Clicking a partially visible tab then reveals it along with part of the next one, so mouse users without a horizontal scroll wheel can still reach every tab. It also covers selection without focus, such as find-in-page.

#### Fading the edges

Fade the content with a mask on the scroller itself: an opaque layer minus a gradient band at each end. Masking only the bands keeps everything else visible, including a focus outline on the scroller, which a single gradient clipped to the border box would hide. Animate each band's size with a scroll-driven animation on the scroller's own scroll position: each fade grows from 0 as the user scrolls away from that end, and shrinks back to 0 as they reach the other end. When the content fits, the scroll timeline is inactive and there is no fade.

```css
@property --fade-start {
  syntax: "<length>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 0px;
}

@property --fade-end {
  syntax: "<length>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 0px;
}

.scroller {
  overflow-x: auto;
  /* Example value: how far each fade reaches */
  --fade-size: 3rem;
  /* Gradient directions and positions are physical, so flip them for right-to-left content */
  --fade-dir: right;
  --fade-start-pos: 0;
  --fade-end-pos: 100%;
  mask:
    linear-gradient(black, black) no-clip subtract,
    linear-gradient(to var(--fade-dir), black, transparent) var(--fade-start-pos) 0 / var(--fade-start) 100% no-repeat no-clip,
    linear-gradient(to var(--fade-dir), transparent, black) var(--fade-end-pos) 0 / var(--fade-end) 100% no-repeat no-clip;
  animation: reveal-fade-start linear both, hide-fade-end linear both;
  animation-timeline: scroll(self inline);
  /* Each fade animates over the first or last --fade-size of scrolling */
  animation-range: 0 var(--fade-size), calc(100% - var(--fade-size)) 100%;

  &:dir(rtl) {
    --fade-dir: left;
    --fade-start-pos: 100%;
    --fade-end-pos: 0;
  }
}

@keyframes reveal-fade-start {
  to {
    --fade-start: var(--fade-size);
  }
}

@keyframes hide-fade-end {
  from {
    --fade-end: var(--fade-size);
  }
}
```

- MANDATORY: Register `--fade-start` and `--fade-end` with `@property`. Unregistered custom properties do not interpolate, so the fades would jump.
- Set `scroll-padding-inline` (or `scroll-padding-block`) to the fade size if the scroller uses scroll snapping or contains focusable items, so snapped and focused items never stop inside a fade.
- For vertical scrollers, use `to bottom`, put the bands at `0 0` and `0 100%` with sizes `100% var(--fade-start)` and `100% var(--fade-end)`, and use `scroll(self block)` and `overflow-y`.
- For right-to-left content, flip `--fade-dir` and swap the band positions, as in the `:dir(rtl)` rule above. The scroll timeline follows the writing direction, but gradient directions and mask positions are physical.

### Strategy 2: overflow menu

Hide the tabs that don’t fit and list them in a menu opened by a “More” button after the tablist.
Build the button and its menu as a menu button; see `menu` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "menu"`) for structure, positioning, and keyboard behavior.
The rest of this section covers only what is specific to tabs.

- **Place the “More” button outside the tablist.** A tablist may only contain tabs. This makes it a separate `Tab` stop that arrow keys do not reach. If arrow-key access matters more, move focus from the last visible tab to the “More” button on `ArrowRight`/`End`, and back on `ArrowLeft`. The tablist itself does nothing at its last tab, so this does not conflict with it, but it is custom key handling that users of the tab pattern may not expect.
- **Make the “More” button look different from a tab.** Use an icon (such as a horizontal ellipsis) with an accessible name, not a text label: a text label looks like one more tab.
- **Detect overflow with `IntersectionObserver`**, rooted at the tablist with `threshold: 1`, so partially visible tabs count as overflowed. It reports each tab as it starts or stops fitting, with no layout math.
- **Hide overflowed tabs with `visibility: hidden`**, not `display: none` or `hidden`. They keep their layout box, so the observer still reports when they fit again, and they leave the focus order and the accessibility tree. Set `overflow: clip` on the tablist so they don’t overflow the page.
- **Mirror each tab as a `menuitem`**. Show only the items whose tab is hidden. Activating an item selects its tab and focuses it. Focus the tab before hiding the popover: otherwise the popover returns focus to the previously focused tab, which selects it again.
- **Hide the “More” button when no tab overflows.** This cannot loop: showing the button only narrows the tablist, so at least one tab still overflows.
- **Keep the selected tab in the tablist.** DO NOT leave it in the menu or mark the “More” button as selected: it is a button, not a tab. When the selected tab overflows (selected from the menu, by find-in-page, or after a resize), set `display: none` on the tabs before it, closest first, until it fits. The observer then reports them as overflowed, so they appear in the menu. DO NOT reorder tabs (in the DOM or with CSS `order`): the removed tabs are gone from both the visual and the focus order, so the two still match. Keep overflowed tabs visible while they are selected or focused: a selected tab can then be focused before the observer catches up, and a focused tab keeps focus when a resize overflows it.

```html
<div class="tabs">
  <div class="tablist" focusgroup="tablist nomemory" aria-label="Product details">
    <button type="button">Overview</button>
    <button type="button">Specifications</button>
    <!-- … -->
  </div>
  <!-- Outside the tablist: a tablist may only contain tabs.
       Icon, not text, so it does not read as another tab. -->
  <button type="button" class="more" hidden aria-label="More tabs" aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false">⋯</button>
  <!-- Menu: see the menu guide. One menuitem per tab. -->
  <!-- Tab panels -->
</div>
```

```js
const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
  for (const { target: tab, intersectionRatio } of entries) {
    const overflowed = intersectionRatio < 1;
    // CSS: .clipped:not([aria-selected="true"], :focus) { visibility: hidden; }
    tab.classList.toggle('clipped', overflowed);
    menuItemFor(tab).hidden = !overflowed;
  }
  more.hidden = !tabs.some(tab => tab.classList.contains('clipped'));
  reveal();
}, { root: tablist, threshold: 1 });

tabs.forEach(tab => observer.observe(tab));

// Also call after every selection change.
// CSS: .displaced { display: none; }
function reveal() {
  const tab = tabs.find(tab => tab.ariaSelected === 'true');
  const before = tabs.slice(0, tabs.indexOf(tab));
  tabs.forEach(tab => tab.classList.remove('displaced'));
  while (before.length && !fits(tab)) {
    before.pop().classList.add('displaced');
  }
}

// Checks both edges, so it works in either writing direction
function fits(tab) {
  const list = tablist.getBoundingClientRect();
  const { left, right } = tab.getBoundingClientRect();
  return left >= list.left && right <= list.right;
}
```

### Strategy 3: Making inactive tabs smaller

This may be combined with the overflow handling strategies described above to allow more tabs to fit before scrolling or showing a menu.
It is more well suited to user-driven tablists (e.g. a document editor) rather than hardcoded UI tablists.

- Let inactive tabs shrink and keep the selected tab at its full width: `flex: 0 1 auto` on the tabs, and `min-inline-size: max-content` on the selected tab, which also overrides any `max-inline-size`, so its label is never truncated. DO NOT use `flex-shrink: 0` for this: it cannot transition smoothly (see below).
- Set a `min-inline-size` on the tabs. Once every inactive tab is at its minimum, the remaining overflow is handled by scrolling or the overflow menu.
- Truncate shrunk labels with `text-overflow: ellipsis`. Put the label in its own element, so `overflow: hidden` applies to the text only and an icon beside it is never clipped.
- Keep the full label in the DOM, so the accessible name and find-in-page are unaffected. Reveal it by scrolling the label inside the tab on hover, and on focus when activation is manual (with automatic activation the focused tab is selected, so it is already full width). See `single-line-overflow` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "single-line-overflow"`) for more details.
- If tabs have icons, keep the icon visible and truncate only the label, so a minimum-width tab is still recognizable.
- Transition `min-inline-size` so tabs resize smoothly when the selection changes, instead of jumping. Set `interpolate-size: allow-keywords` on the tablist so it interpolates to and from `max-content`; no view transition is needed. Only transition it when `prefers-reduced-motion` is `no-preference`. Transitioning `flex-shrink` does not work: any value above 0 shrinks a tab all the way to its minimum when the tabs overflow.



## Fallback strategies

### Fallbacks & browser support for focusgroup

Browser support for focusgroup: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 150 (Jun 2026) and Edge 150 (Jul 2026).
Unsupported in: Firefox and Safari.

Load `@microsoft/focusgroup-polyfill` only when native support is missing:

```js
if (!(Object.hasOwn(HTMLElement.prototype, 'focusGroup'))) {
  const { polyfill } = await import('@microsoft/focusgroup-polyfill');
  polyfill();
}
```

- Use the `focusgroup` and `focusgroupstart` attributes, not the `focusGroup` property, which the polyfill does not add.
- The polyfill does not notice items hidden or shown later: add `focusgroup="none"` to an item while it is hidden.

### Fallbacks & browser support for `hidden=until-found`

Browser support for hidden="until-found": Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 102 (May 2022), Edge 102 (May 2022), and Firefox 148 (Feb 2026).
Unsupported in: Safari.

Treat `hidden=until-found` as an enhancement. Do not polyfill it: find-in-page cannot be reproduced across browsers.
No feature detection is needed when setting it from script: in browsers without support, `panel.hidden = 'until-found'` sets the boolean `hidden`, so the panel is simply hidden and not searchable.

### Fallbacks & browser support for `interpolate-size`

Browser support for interpolate-size: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 129 (Sep 2024) and Edge 129 (Sep 2024).
Unsupported in: Firefox and Safari.

No fallback is needed: without `interpolate-size`, tabs resize instantly instead of transitioning.

### Fallbacks & browser support for scroll-driven animations

Baseline status for Scroll-driven animations: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2026-10-27.
Supported by: Chrome 115 (Jul 2023), Edge 115 (Jul 2023), Firefox 159 (Oct 2026), and Safari 26 (Sep 2025).

Without scroll-driven animations, the fades are never shown, and neither are the shadows if scroll-state queries are also unsupported. Use one of these last-resort fallbacks.

#### Shadows on a solid background: background attachment

If the scroller has a solid background color, draw the shadows with backgrounds instead. This works in every browser and needs no JavaScript.
Each shadow is a `scroll` background, which stays at the edge of the scrollport.
Each shadow has a cover: a `local` background in the scroller's own color, which scrolls with the content.
At either end of the content its cover sits on top of its shadow and hides it; once the user scrolls away from that end, the cover moves away and the shadow shows.

```css
@supports not ((container-type: scroll-state) or (animation-timeline: scroll())) {
  .scroller {
    background:
      /* Covers: scroll with the content. Must use the scroller's own background color. */
      linear-gradient(var(--surface) 30%, transparent) top / 100% 2.5rem no-repeat local,
      linear-gradient(transparent, var(--surface) 70%) bottom / 100% 2.5rem no-repeat local,
      /* Shadows: stay at the edges of the scrollport */
      radial-gradient(farthest-side at 50% 0, rgb(0 0 0 / .15), transparent) top / 100% .75rem no-repeat,
      radial-gradient(farthest-side at 50% 100%, rgb(0 0 0 / .15), transparent) bottom / 100% .75rem no-repeat,
      var(--surface);
  }
}
```

The shadows paint behind the content, so content with its own opaque background hides them. In that case, use the `IntersectionObserver` fallback below.

#### Fades, or shadows on any background: `IntersectionObserver`

Use an `IntersectionObserver` to mark which edges have more content, and show the hints from those attributes. Observe the scroller's first and last children, with the scroller as the root and `threshold: 1`: a child that is not fully visible means there is more content beyond it.

```js
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
  for (const scroller of document.querySelectorAll('.scroller')) {
    const [first, last] = [scroller.firstElementChild, scroller.lastElementChild];
    // rootMargin: without it, sub-pixel widths can leave an edge child a fraction of a pixel short of fully visible
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
      for (const { target, intersectionRatio } of entries) {
        scroller.classList.toggle(target === first ? 'more-start' : 'more-end', intersectionRatio < 1);
      }
    }, { root: scroller, threshold: 1, rootMargin: '1px' });

    observer.observe(first);
    observer.observe(last);
  }
}
```

```css
/* Shadows */
.scroller.more-start::before,
.scroller.more-end::after {
  opacity: 1;
}

/* Fades */
.scroller {
  transition: --fade-start .2s, --fade-end .2s;

  &.more-start {
    --fade-start: var(--fade-size);
  }

  &.more-end {
    --fade-end: var(--fade-size);
  }
}
```

If a single child can be larger than the scroller (e.g. one long article), it is never fully visible, so this would always show both hints. Observe sentinels instead: empty elements that the script adds at each end of the scroller. A sentinel that is out of view means there is more content beyond it.

```js
// Must be valid children of the scroller (e.g. <li aria-hidden="true"> in a list)
const [start, end] = [document.createElement('div'), document.createElement('div')];
for (const sentinel of [start, end]) {
  // A zero-size sentinel at the very edge never counts as intersecting
  sentinel.style.cssText = 'min-inline-size: 1px; min-block-size: 1px';
}
scroller.prepend(start);
scroller.append(end);
// Observe them as above, with the default threshold, using !isIntersecting instead of intersectionRatio < 1
```

# Maximize content and reduce noise

Users come for the content. Keep the page frame small, move it out of the way while users read, and show secondary content only when asked.

## Implementation

### Don't block or interrupt the user

- DO NOT open newsletter modals, interstitials, or full-screen promos when the page loads. Where possible, remove banners and unrelated content that compete with the main content.
- If a notice is legally required (for example, cookie consent), show it as a small, non-modal bar that covers well under 15% of the viewport, with a visible dismiss button labeled with text such as "Accept" or "Close".
- Match the interruption to the stakes: use a blocking dialog only for consequential actions, such as deleting an account, paying, or editing personal data. Use non-blocking messages for everything else.
- Don't move content while the user reads. Reserve space for images, ads, and embeds before they load (for example, with `width` and `height` attributes), don't insert content above what's on screen, and don't open popovers the user didn't ask for.

### Keep persistent bars compact

Keep every fixed or sticky bar under 12% of the viewport height. Use a single row, and don't stack several sticky bars (announcement, header, and sub-navigation). Move extra items into a popover menu or drawer instead (see below).

### Hide persistent bars while the user scrolls down

Hide the top bar (the site header or navigation bar) while the user scrolls down and bring it back as soon as they scroll up, so it's out of the way while reading but one flick away. A scroll-state container query tracks the scroll direction in CSS, with no script. Hide bottom bars, such as a tab bar, the same way, sliding them down instead of up.

```css
/* The root scroller remembers the direction of the most recent scroll. */
html {
  container-type: scroll-state;
}
.top-bar {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: 1; /* Example value: keeps positioned content from painting over the bar. */
}
/* Scrolling down: move the bar out of view. transform doesn't change layout, so content doesn't jump.
   (scrollable: top) shows the bar at the very top, even after jumps such as the Home key, which don't update "scrolled". */
@container scroll-state((scrolled: bottom) and (scrollable: top)) {
  .top-bar {
    transform: translateY(-100%);
  }
}
/* Keep the bar visible while it contains keyboard focus. */
.top-bar:focus-within {
  transform: none;
}
/* Example value: at least the bar's height, so in-page link targets don't land under the bar. */
:target {
  scroll-margin-top: 4rem;
}
```

Alternatively, shrink the bar to under 80% of its height as the user scrolls; see `shrinking-header-on-scroll` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "shrinking-header-on-scroll"`).

### Put secondary UI in popovers and drawers

Use the `popover` attribute for menus, overflow actions, tooltips, hover cards, and onboarding tips instead of permanent toolbars or custom JavaScript overlays. A button with `popovertarget` opens and closes the popover on click, <kbd>Enter</kbd>, or <kbd>Space</kbd>. The default `popover="auto"` also closes it on <kbd>Esc</kbd> or a click outside.

```html
<button popovertarget="more-links">More</button>
<!-- Rendered in the top layer, so no z-index management is needed. -->
<nav id="more-links" popover aria-label="More links">
  <a href="/about">About</a>
  <a href="/contact">Contact</a>
</nav>
```

- Popovers open in the center of the screen by default. To place a popover next to its button, see `viewport-aware-dropdowns` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "viewport-aware-dropdowns"`).
- Buttons can also open popovers with `commandfor` and `command="toggle-popover"`; see `declarative-dialog-popover-control` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "declarative-dialog-popover-control"`).
- For larger sets of options, use an off-canvas drawer: a `popover` panel pinned to one side of the screen that slides in over the page (see `navigation-drawer` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "navigation-drawer"`) for swipe-to-close and animation). Show rows of related items as a horizontally scrolling carousel instead of a long stacked list.

### Collapse secondary content with `details`

Put long or rarely needed content (specs, FAQs, fine print) in a `details` element. It starts closed, and its `summary` works with the keyboard and screen readers without JavaScript.

```html
<details>
  <summary>Shipping and returns</summary>
  <p>Free returns within 30 days.</p>
</details>
```

## Fallback strategies

### Scroll-state queries

Browser support for Container scroll-state queries: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 144 (Jan 2026) and Edge 144 (Jan 2026).
Unsupported in: Firefox and Safari.

Without scroll-state queries, the bar never hides, but it stays compact and usable. If your Baseline target includes those browsers, add a small script so the bar hides there too. Browsers that support other scroll-state queries but not `scrolled` also keep the bar visible.

```css
/* Don't hide the bar while it contains keyboard focus. */
.top-bar.is-hidden:not(:focus-within) {
  transform: translateY(-100%);
}
```

```js
// Runs only where scroll-state queries aren't supported. Run it after .top-bar is parsed (for example, in a module script).
if (!CSS.supports('container-type', 'scroll-state')) {
  const bar = document.querySelector('.top-bar');
  let lastY = scrollY;
  addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    // Clamp so rubber-band overscroll at the top or bottom doesn't flip the scroll direction.
    const y = Math.max(0, Math.min(scrollY, document.documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight));
    if (y === lastY) return;
    // Hide only after scrolling down past the bar's own height, so it doesn't flicker near the top.
    bar?.classList.toggle('is-hidden', y > lastY && y > bar.offsetHeight);
    lastY = y;
  }, { passive: true });
}
```

### Fallbacks & browser support for Popover

Baseline status for Popover: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2025-01-27.
Supported by: Chrome 116 (Aug 2023), Edge 116 (Aug 2023), Firefox 125 (Apr 2024), Safari 17 (Sep 2023), and Safari iOS 18.3 (Jan 2025).

The Popover API is mostly **progressive enhancement**, but its defining behaviors — top-layer promotion, light-dismiss, and `popovertarget` invocation — have no CSS-only equivalent. Older browsers need a polyfill, or a manual fallback if you would rather not ship one.

**Polyfill:** To support the `popover` attribute in older browsers, conditionally load [`@oddbird/popover-polyfill`](https://github.com/oddbird/popover-polyfill). **MANDATORY:** Feature detect by checking for the `popover` property on `HTMLElement.prototype`, and load the polyfill **only** when native support is missing — do NOT load it unconditionally.

With a bundler or import map:

```js
// MANDATORY: Feature detect 'popover' on HTMLElement.prototype.
if (!Object.hasOwn(HTMLElement.prototype, "popover")) {
  import("@oddbird/popover-polyfill");
}
```

If not using a package manager, import from a CDN, e.g. `"https://unpkg.com/@oddbird/popover-polyfill@latest/dist/popover.min.js"`.

Caveats when using the polyfill:
- Do NOT use the `popover` IDL attribute for any code that may run before the polyfill is fully loaded. It will not be mirrored as an attribute, the popover will not be picked up by the polyfill, and the property will be shadowing the accessor the polyfill adds forever. Use `setAttribute()`/`removeAttribute()` instead.
- **Styling caveat:** The polyfill cannot define the real `:popover-open` pseudo-class, so it applies a `.\:popover-open` class instead. **MANDATORY:** Combine the two with `:is()` or `:where()`, otherwise browsers that lack `:popover-open` discard the entire rule:

```css
[popover]:is(:popover-open, .\:popover-open) {
  display: block;
}
```

### Invoker commands

Baseline status for Invoker commands: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2025-12-12.
Supported by: Chrome 135 (Apr 2025), Edge 135 (Apr 2025), Firefox 144 (Oct 2025), and Safari 26.2 (Dec 2025).

Browsers without invoker commands ignore `commandfor`, so the button does nothing there. Prefer `popovertarget`, which works wherever popovers do. If you use `commandfor`, dynamically import `invokers-polyfill` (through your bundler, or from a CDN if you don't use one) only when `'commandForElement' in HTMLButtonElement.prototype` is false.

# Adapt to the form factor

The same interface runs on phones, laptops, and foldables, with touch, mouse, or keyboard. Size controls for the input, let components adapt to their container, and keep text and controls clear of system UI.

For page-level responsive techniques (dynamic viewport units, aspect ratios, and fluid type), see `responsive-design` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "responsive-design"`).

## Implementation

### Size targets for the input method

Make every control at least 24×24 CSS pixels (WCAG 2.5.8), and at least 44×44 on touch screens (WCAG 2.5.5). Links inside sentences are exempt. For checkboxes and radios, wrap the input in its `<label>` and size the label. To keep small icons visually compact, extend their hit area with a pseudo-element. Leave enough space around controls so extended hit areas do not overlap each other or reach past the edge of the screen.

```css
:root { --target-size: 24px; }
@media (pointer: coarse) {
  :root { --target-size: 44px; }
}
:where(button, select, textarea, input:not([type="checkbox"], [type="radio"]), [role="button"], nav a, label:has(input[type="checkbox"], input[type="radio"])) {
  min-block-size: var(--target-size);
  min-inline-size: var(--target-size);
}
:where([role="button"], nav a, label:has(input[type="checkbox"], input[type="radio"])) { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; }
.icon-button { position: relative; }
.icon-button::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -10px;
}
```

### Let components respond to their container

Use container queries instead of viewport-width media queries for component layout, so a component adapts wherever it is placed. Keep media queries for what a container can't know: the input device (such as `pointer: coarse` for touch screens) and user settings (such as `prefers-color-scheme`).

```css
.product-list {
  /* Name containers so queries inside nested containers target the right ancestor. */
  container: products / inline-size;
}
/* A container query styles the container's descendants, never the container itself. */
@container products (inline-size > 40rem) {
  .product-card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr; }
}
```

DO NOT query `height` or `block-size` on an `inline-size` container: it measures only width. Use `container-type: size` only on a container with a definite height.

- For more container query patterns, such as card layouts, see `size-aware-styling` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "size-aware-styling"`).
- To change a component's layout based on what child elements it contains (for example, switching a card to a side-by-side layout when it includes an image), see `content-based-styling` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "content-based-styling"`).

### Keep UI clear of notches and system bars

When drawing edge to edge with `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">`, pad fixed and edge-anchored UI by the safe-area insets.

```css
.bottom-bar {
  position: fixed;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  padding-bottom: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
  padding-left: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px));
  padding-right: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px));
}
```

Pad UI anchored to the top edge the same way with `safe-area-inset-top`.

### Balance headings and prettify body text

Apply `text-wrap: balance` to short headings (`h1, h2, h3`) and `text-wrap: pretty` to body text (`p, li`) to prevent typographic orphans. For full text-wrapping rules, see `improve-text-layout-and-legibility` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "improve-text-layout-and-legibility"`). DO NOT apply either property to `*`: both incur layout overhead, and `balance` only helps short blocks of text.

### Label icon-only buttons

Give every icon-only button an accessible name using `aria-label`, an `<img alt>`, or visually hidden text, and hide decorative icons with `aria-hidden="true"`. For more accessible naming patterns, see `accessibility` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "accessibility"`).

### Keep content inside its box

Ensure text and controls are never clipped, squished to zero dimensions, or overflowing at any viewport width down to 320 CSS pixels (WCAG 1.4.10).

```css
/* Media never overflows its column. */
img, video { max-inline-size: 100%; block-size: auto; }
/* Long words and URLs wrap instead of widening the page. */
body { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
/* Flex and grid items don't shrink below their content by default. Let non-control items shrink so controls keep their minimum size. */
:where(.toolbar > :not(button, input, select, textarea, a, [role="button"], label)) { min-inline-size: 0; }
.toolbar { display: flex; }
/* The target-size minimum lets controls in a crowded row shrink below their text, so let nav bars and toolbars that are already rows wrap. */
:where(nav, nav ul, .toolbar) { flex-wrap: wrap; }
```

- Use `min-block-size` instead of a fixed `height` on anything that contains text.
- DO NOT truncate headings with `text-overflow: ellipsis`, and do not clip controls with `overflow: hidden` on their parents.
- To allow text and layouts to scale with operating system accessibility settings, see `respect-os-text-scale` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "respect-os-text-scale"`). To size form controls dynamically as users type, see `form-fields-automatically-fit-contents` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "form-fields-automatically-fit-contents"`).

## Fallback strategies

### Text wrapping

Baseline status for text-wrap: balance: Newly available. It's been Baseline since 2024-05-13.
Supported by: Chrome 114 (May 2023), Edge 114 (Jun 2023), Firefox 121 (Dec 2023), and Safari 17.5 (May 2024).

Browser support for text-wrap: pretty: Limited availability.
Supported by: Chrome 117 (Sep 2023), Edge 117 (Sep 2023), and Safari 26 (Sep 2025).
Unsupported in: Firefox.

Browsers without support ignore `text-wrap: balance` and `text-wrap: pretty` and wrap text normally. No fallback is needed.

# Implement natural interactions

Interfaces feel natural when every input gets an immediate response, motion behaves like a physical object, content follows the user's finger, and nothing depends on a mouse.

## Implementation

### Give every control hover, press, and focus feedback

```css
:where(button) {
  /* example durations */
  transition: box-shadow 150ms ease-out, scale 150ms ease-out;
}
button:hover { box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2); }
a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
button:active { scale: 0.97; } /* example value */

:where(a, button, input, select, textarea, summary, [tabindex]):focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid CanvasText;
  outline-offset: 3px;
}
```

- Use `:focus-visible` so keyboard users always see a clear focus ring, without showing it on mouse clicks.
- DO NOT remove focus outlines (`outline: none` or `outline: 0`) without providing an equally visible replacement with at least a 3:1 contrast ratio against the page (WCAG 1.4.11). `CanvasText` is the text color for the page's `color-scheme`, so it contrasts with the default page background in light mode, in dark mode (with `color-scheme: light dark`), and in forced colors. If you set your own page background, pick a ring color with 3:1 against it. `currentColor` fails when a button's text color matches the page.
- Make each interaction state look different, including combinations: hover, press, pressing while hovering, and keyboard focus. In the example, hover adds a shadow, press a scale, and focus an outline, so any combination shows each of its parts.

### Open disclosures from their trigger

Popovers and dropdowns should visibly open from the control that triggered them: scale out from the corner touching the trigger, or slide out from its side. Menus and hovercards have their own open motion; follow `menu` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "menu"`) and `hovercard` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "hovercard"`). For other popovers, place the popover next to its trigger and align `transform-origin` with the corner that touches it. To flip dropdowns at viewport edges, see `viewport-aware-dropdowns` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "viewport-aware-dropdowns"`).

```css
.dropdown {
  /* popovertarget makes the trigger the popover's anchor. */
  position-area: bottom span-right;
  inset: auto;
  margin: 0;
  /* The corner touching the trigger; change it whenever the placement changes. */
  transform-origin: top left;
}
/* example durations */
.dropdown:is(:popover-open, .\:popover-open) { animation: fade-in 150ms ease-out both; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .dropdown:is(:popover-open, .\:popover-open) {
    /* optional example spring curve, written inline */
    animation: grow 300ms linear(0, 0.45 15%, 1.08 35%, 0.97 55%, 1.01 75%, 1) both, fade-in 150ms ease-out both;
  }
}
@keyframes grow { from { scale: 0.9; } }
@keyframes fade-in { from { opacity: 0; } }
```

- Keep animations on the compositor (`transform`, its individual properties, and `opacity`) and respect reduced-motion preferences; see `motion` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "motion"`). This example animates the opening only; for exit transitions, see `animate-to-from-top-layer` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "animate-to-from-top-layer"`).
- Trigger popovers declaratively using `<button popovertarget="dropdown-id">` (see `declarative-dialog-popover-control` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "declarative-dialog-popover-control"`)) to get native click, keyboard, and dismiss behavior without JavaScript.
- For a springy open, use a `linear()` curve that overshoots slightly before settling (an output value above 1); see `physics-based-easing` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "physics-based-easing"`).

### Make horizontal scrollers swipeable

Any horizontally scrollable row of items, such as a carousel, gallery, or row of chips, should scroll natively and snap to its items.

```css
.scroller {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
  overflow-x: auto;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
}
.scroller > * {
  flex: 0 0 85%; /* example item width */
  scroll-snap-align: start;
}
```

- Native overflow scrolling gives swiping its physics and momentum, and snapping makes each swipe come to rest on an item, with no touch listeners.
- If none of the scroller's items contains a link, button, or other focusable element, make the scroller itself focusable and labeled (`<div class="scroller" role="region" aria-label="Featured items" tabindex="0">`), so keyboard users can reach it and scroll it with the arrow keys. When items are focusable, tabbing to them already scrolls them into view.
- To highlight the item that snaps into place, see `carousel-snap-highlights` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "carousel-snap-highlights"`). To swipe a list item aside to reveal actions, see `swipe-to-reveal` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "swipe-to-reveal"`). For side menus that close when swiped away, see `navigation-drawer` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "navigation-drawer"`).
- For carousel controls such as previous and next buttons and scroll markers, see `carousel` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "carousel"`).

### Add motion blur to large, fast movements (optional)

For large, rapid translations (such as a full-screen sheet sliding into view), a subtle, temporary blur softens the movement. `filter` doesn't run only on the compositor, so keep blur to large, brief movements, skip it for small controls or slow transitions, and always guard it with a reduced-motion media query.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .sheet.is-open { animation: sheet-in 300ms ease-out both; } /* example duration */
}
@keyframes sheet-in {
  from { translate: 0 100%; filter: blur(6px); } /* example blur radius */
}
```

### Make every action reachable without a mouse

- Use `<button>` for actions and `<a>` with an `href` for navigation. Both receive keyboard focus and activate on <kbd>Enter</kbd>; buttons also activate on <kbd>Space</kbd>.
- Menus and dropdowns may open on hover, but never only on hover: they also open on click, tap, and keyboard activation, stay open while the pointer moves onto them, and close with <kbd>Esc</kbd> (WCAG 2.1.1, 1.4.13).
- Controls or details revealed in place on hover, such as card actions, must also appear on keyboard focus and be visible by default on devices without hover:

```css
.card .actions {
  opacity: 0;

  .card:hover &,
  .card:focus-within & { opacity: 1; }

  @media (hover: none) { opacity: 1; }
}
```

For tooltips and previews triggered on hover, focus, or long-press, see `interest-triggered-tooltips` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "interest-triggered-tooltips"`) and `interest-triggered-action-previews` (via `npx -y modern-web-guidance@latest retrieve "interest-triggered-action-previews"`).

